How to Turn Homestead Projects into High School Credits in Missouri
<span style="color:#d84315;display:block;font-size:.6em;font-weight:400;margin-top:5px;">A Practical Guide to Mapping, Documenting &amp; Building Academic Credit</span>
</h1>

<div style="background:#fff3e0;border-left:5px solid #d84315;border-radius:6px;margin:0 0 30px;padding:18px 20px;">
<strong>Important Missouri-law note:</strong> Missouri's current home-school record and hour requirements are found in <strong>RSMo §167.012</strong>. The statute addresses a 1,000-hour framework, including at least 600 hours in specified academic areas or related academic courses and at least 400 of those 600 hours at the regular home-school location for students to whom the applicable subsection applies. It also identifies written records, portfolios, and evaluations—or equivalent credible evidence—as documentation of regular instruction. This article is an educational planning resource, not legal advice. Always verify the current statute for your student's situation.
</div>

<p>For families who learn through gardening, animal care, building, preserving food, maintaining systems, and solving real-world problems, the boundary between daily life and intentional education can be wonderfully small.</p>
<p>The trick is not simply calling a chore “school.” The stronger approach is to identify the <strong>academic work inside the project</strong>, document what was learned, and save evidence that shows the student's thinking and progress.</p>

<div style="background:#f1f8e9;border-left:5px solid rgb(129,199,132);border-radius:4px;margin:30px 0;padding:20px;">
<h2 style="color:#2e7d32;font-size:1.8em;margin-top:0;">1. Understand the Current Missouri Framework</h2>
<p>Under current RSMo §167.012, applicable Missouri home schools generally document regular instruction through a written record of subjects and activities, a portfolio of academic work, and evaluations of academic progress—or credible evidence equivalent to those records.</p>
<ul>
<li>☀️ <strong>1,000 hours:</strong> Minimum instruction offered under the applicable subsection.</li>
<li>📚 <strong>600 hours:</strong> At least 600 must be reading, language arts, mathematics, social studies, science, or related academic courses consistent with the child's age and ability.</li>
<li>🏠 <strong>400 of those 600:</strong> Must occur at the regular home-school location.</li>
<li>📝 <strong>Records:</strong> Keep a plan book/diary or equivalent written record, portfolio samples, and evaluations—or equivalent credible evidence.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> The law does not create a separate statutory bucket called “400 non-core/elective hours.” The remaining hours can include other learning, but families should document the actual instruction rather than treating the 400 as a legally defined elective category.</p>
<p>For high-school planning, RSMo §167.031 also contains a definition of a completed credit as 100 hours or more of instruction in a course for the purposes described in that section. That is not the same as saying every 100 hours automatically becomes a transcript credit.</p>
